o & Min edge  | 352,663,012  | 66.32%  | 11.21%  | 33.68%\nL310: Low-textual images  | 352,204,550  | 66.36%  | 0.13%  | 33.64%\nL311: Blurry images  | 333,255,945  | 68.17%  | 5.38%  | 31.83%\nL312: Entropy  | 316,359,868  | 69.79%  | 5.07%  | 30.21%\nL313: Visual redundancy  | 186,019,602  | 82.23%  | 41.20%  | 17.77%\nL314: NSFW  | 178,601,215  | 82.94%  | 3.25%  | 17.06%\nL315: Cross-modality Filtering  | 154,293,590  | 85.26%  | 13.61%  | 14.74%\nL316: Cross-batch Visual Redundancy  | 99,892,381  | 90.46%  | 35.26%  | 9.54%\nL317: Final image-text pairs  | 99,892,381  | 90.46%  | –  | 9.54%\nL318: Table 6: Specific statistics information of the DanQing dataset construction pipeline.\nL319: We further illustrate the statistical breakdown of our data construction pipeline in Tab. cite222†6 . Starting with an initial collection of approximately 1B raw image-text URLs, we apply a multi-stage refinement process, comprising coarse- and fine-grained filtering, cross-modality filtering, and deduplication. This pipeline ultimately yields nearly 100M high-quality image-text pairs (storage space occupies approximately 12TB), effectively filtering out 90% of the original noise to ensure data quality.\nL320: ## Appendix B Examples in DanQing Dataset\nL321: \nL322: cite223†Image: Refer to caption Figure 9: Visualization of image-text pairs in DanQing dataset.\nL323: Fig. cite224†9 presents representative examples from the DanQing dataset, comprising images and their corresponding Chinese textual descriptions. Specifically, these image-text pairs encompass a wide range of domains, such as natural scenery, historical literature, and automotive technology, demonstrating the thematic diversity of our dataset. This breadth makes DanQing particularly well-suited for general-purpose Vision-Language Pre-training.\nL324: ## Appendix C Exploring DanQing\nL325: ### C.1 Source Domain Distribution\nL326: Rank  | Source Domain  | Total Count  | %  | Categories\nL327: 1  | alicdn.